**************************************************************************
1. Introduction

SCORE is a program to evaluate soaring flight performance recorded by
an FAI approved flight recorder (IGC file).
The source code is ANSI C. SCORE is linked with the standard C and math
libraries and with the plotting library DISLIN to create a PNG image of 
the flight path and the GD library to create a PNG image of the barogram.
Linux distributions of DISLIN are available freely from www.dislin.de, 
the source code of GD can be found here: www.boutell.com/gd/

Input:  IGC file and command line options
Output: data file (.dat), text file (.txt) and PNG plots (.png)
        The path of these files is given by the IGC file.



**************************************************************************
2. Usage

SCORE reads the input file name and options from the command line.

SCORE [options] <file name>

  <file name> is the IGC file to read (supposed to end in .igc or .IGC)

  Options:
  -c <finesse>  : the "finesse" (L/D) of the glider (real number)
  -fixm:<xxx>   : limits the number of fixes in any soaring block to the given
                  number, with a minimum of 15000
  -nd           : do not check task declaration
  -nff          : do not perform free flight scoring
  -nft          : do not perform FastTrack scoring
  -ndt          : do not perform declared task scoring
  -ndtime       : do not check task declaration time
  -ntri         : Do not perform triangle scoring
  -nruns        : Do not perform runs scoring (stretches)
  -tricdX.x     : Checks for closed loop, max distance between fixes = X.x (km).
                  X.x = 0: disable closed loop checking
                  Parameter not included: enable close loop, using dist = 1 km.
  -tribf        : Use brute force triangle algorithm, to compare with optimised
  -tribfssXX    : Use brute force triangle algorithm, set step size at XX seconds
  -tribfmnsXX   : Use brute force triangle algorithm, limit number of steps to XX
                  has priority over tribfss
  -nbru         : Do not perform Charronde scoring
  -gid          : do not treat missing of GID record as critical error
  -e            : glider with engine (switches on the engine run check)
  -ea           : Automatic engine detection (as -e, but no error if no ENL)
  -noenl        : Allow analysis of a file without enl registration
  -moponly      : Analyse only files with MOP detection
  -nomop        : Do not use mop detection
  +nt           : require a testrun
  -etmin<xxx>   : use the specified value as minimum for ENL threshold
                  (no space)
				  The default value is 400
  -etmax<xxx>   : use the specified value as maximum for ENL threshold
                  (no space)
				  The default value is 500
  -et<xxx>      : use the specified value as ENL threshold (no space)
  -st hh:mm:ss hh:mm:ss   :
                  Analyse only the block between the given times
                  Engine detection is disabled,
                  Landing detection is disabled.
				  Start soaring (release) is set at the start time
                  The blanks must be inserted!
  -nknicks<xx>  : Set the base number of knicks for free flight analysis
                  Reducing the value will decrease processing time, but
                  will also decrease the quality of the result.
                  Current default value: 18.
                  Minimum value: 14
                  Maximum value: 30
				  The actual number of knicks is about 3x higher as the number given
  -ddmld<xx>    : Free flight minimum leg distance (0->50)
  -dtmld<xx>    : Declared task minimum leg distance (0->50)
  -d            : write debugging messages to stdout
  -digc         : provide debug info for igc validity checking and input
  -dflt         : provide debug info for flight detection algorithm
  -drel         : provide debug info for release detection algorithm
  -deng         : provide debug info for engine detection algorithm
  -dalt         : provide debug info for altitude & altitude gain algorithm 
  -dff          : provide debug info for free flight scoring algorithm
  -ddt          : provide debug info for declared task scoring algorithm
  -dtri         : Provide debug info for triangle algorithm
  -dbru         : Provide debug info for Charronde algorithm
  -druns        : Provide debug info for runs algorithm
  -ddl          : provide debug info for dislin picture generation functions
  -dplot:       : provide debug info for the plots
  -df           : Pipe debug info to specified file. Must be used in
                  combination with another debug parameter (eg -d or -drel)
  -dtimeXX      : Add an execution time of XX seconds, in order to test server
                  behavior in case of long execution times.
  -dttotXX      : Set the execution time to a minimum of the given time XX (seconds).
                  If the execution time is shortre, a waiting time is added
				  to obtain the minimum time.
  -rm<hh:mm:ss> : (no space) Set release point manually at the fix at or
                  just after the given time.
  -ec<:filename>: write list of error codes to file "filename", then stops.
                  If no file name given: writes to stdout.
  -plot:rgn:name: Will produce combined plots with the given name for the given region
                  Multiple instances of -plot commands are allowed
                  > Name: Name of the plot file
				          if containing blanks:can use quotes or underscores:
                            -plot:rgn:"Peeters Jan"
                            -plot:rgn:Peeters_Jan
                  > Regions:
                       BNL
                       FRA
                       EUR
                       MOR:    Morocco
                       SAF:    S Africa
                       USA
                       AUS:    Australia & N Zealand
  -latmax<xx.x> : Specify the top latitiude for the plot
  -latmin<xx.x> : latmax, latmin, lonmax and lonmin must aal be given
  -lonmax<xx.x> : Specify the right longitiude for the plot
  -lonmin<xx.x> : Use degrees.decimal degrees
  -nograph      : Suppresses generation of graphs
  -pause        : request a key press to close the console after execution

Comment out command line parameters via # after the '-' character.
Example: -#plot:xx:xx -> the command is commented out
  
To run the SCORE program on the example IGC file in the distribution:
   
  SCORE -c 47 -e example.igc
  


**************************************************************************
3. Dat file contents
   The dat file contains a series of alfa numerical data, in text format.
   Fields marked with * are new as compared to the previous release
   The data are the following:
   - Version (name) of the scoring algorithm used
   - Unique IGC name, construicted from the logger header.
     If file could not be read or in case of some other errors this field will
     contain the input file name.
   - Critical errors (code + description).
     In case of critical error, the next lines are not included.
   - Number of non-critical errors
     - If errors: list of non critical error codes + descriptions
   - Number of messages
     - If messages: list of message codes + descriptions
   - Logger declaration data:
     - Declaration date
     - Logger code and ID
     - Pilot name
     - Glider type
     - Glider registration
     - Competition class
   - Take off time
   - Landing time
   - Release time
   - Release altitude
   - Flight time
   - Soaring time
   - Altitude gain
   - Declared task data
     - Declared distance
     - Number of turnpoints ( incl take off & ldg )
     - Task finished ?
     - Distance flown
     - Start altitude
     - Altitude at start line crossing
     - Height loss
     - Score distance
     - Average speed
   - Free flight data
     - Total distance
     - Number of turnpoints
     - Height loss
     - Score distance
     - Average speed
   - Best speed track data
     - Best speed
   - Benelux / Take off airfield code
   - Take-off latitude
   - Take-off longitude
   - Landing latitude
   - Landing longitude
   - Distance take-off to landing point
   - Charronde angle.  Can be negative, can be > 360.
   - Triangle data
     - Leg distances (3 lines)
	 - Total distance
	 - Brite force distance. For debugging, will be removed in final version.
   - Up to 10 strings containing debugging info


**************************************************************************
4. Error codes
   See separate file error_codes.txt.
   The error codes file can be genereated via the -ec[:filename] comand switch.
   

**************************************************************************
5. Mutexing principles / problems
   Different instances of score, running the same day, will access the same
   files day_yyyymmdd and day_yyyymmdd.png.  This can lead to file corruption
   or execution problems if no precautions are taken.
   Solution: interprocess mutexing.
   The interprocess mutexing is performed via a locked file, generated by the
   first-come process.  The next process will be unable to generate the same
   file, and enter a wait state.  When the first process, it will delete the
   file, the next process will then be able to create the file itself, and
   proceed.
   If the second process is not able to grab the mutex after 10 seconds, an
   error condition is presumed.  This may occur when the first process exits
   abnormally before it was able to release the mutex.
   In this case, the second process will continue anyhow, and it will try to
   delete the mutex file itself.
   Due to access rights in Linux, this may not be possible.
   *****************************************************************************
   * IF THE EXECUTION OF THE ALGORITHM BECOMES VERY SLOW:                      *
   * It is possible that the mutex file was not deleted by the first process,  *
   * and that the next processes are unable to delete it, due to access rights.*
   * In this case, locate the file Mutex_day_yyyymmdd in the score directory,  *
   * and remove it manually.                                                   *
   *****************************************************************************

   
**************************************************************************
6. Installation of DISLIN
a) Download the appropriate tar.gz distribution here:
   http://www.mps.mpg.de/dislin/linux
b) DISLIN can be installed in a local directory, e.g. $HOME/dislin
c) Define the environment variable DISLIN in the shell startup file, 
   e.g. in $HOME/.cshrc
   setenv DISLIN $home/dislin
d) Follow the installation instructions in the DISLIN README file.
e) Check if the file gshhs_h.b or gshhs_l.h is installed in
   ~/dislin/map.  If not download & install it manually.


**************************************************************************
7. Installation of ZLIB (if zlib is not installed)
a) create a directory $HOME/zlib
b) Download the latest version from http://www.zlib.net/,
c) Copy the package into the folder
d) Unpack archive:  tar -xvf zlibxxx.tar.gz
e) cd to the created directory
f) type: ./configure
g) type: make
h) type: sudo make install
i) optionally: make clean


**************************************************************************
8. Installation of LIBPNG (if libpng is not installed)
a) Make sure zlib is installed. If not, see above.
b) create a directory $HOME/libpng
c) Download the latest version from the libpng website
d) Copy the package into the folder libpng
e) Unpack archive:  tar -xvf libpngxxx.tar.gz
f) cd to the just created libpng folder
g) type: ./configure
h) type: make
i) type: sudo make install
j) optionally: make clean


**************************************************************************
9. Installation of GD
a) Make sure libpng is installed
   To check use "sudo find / -name libpng*"
   If not, see above.
b) create a directory (eg) $HOME/libgd
c) Download the latest tar.gz distribution of the source here:
   http://www.libgd.org/Main_Page
d) Copy the package into the folder libgd
e) Unpack archive:  tar -xvf libgdxxx.tar.gz
f) cd to the just created libpng folder
g) Type: ./configure --with-png
h) Type: make
i) Type: sudo make install
j) optionally: make clean


**************************************************************************
10. Installation of SCORE

a) Uncompress the tar.gz distribution

   gunzip score-0.5.3.tar.gz
   
b) Restore source files from the tar file

   tar xvf score-0.5.3.tar 
   
c) Change to the installation directory of SCORE

   cd score-0.5.3
   
d) If the installation directory is different from $home/score-0.5.3,
   edit the Makefile and change the variable PROJ_DIR accordingly
   
e) Compile and link SCORE with the command

   make
   
f) Copy the executable SCORE and the files "airfields_*.dat" from 
   the current directory to the execution directory, e.g. $home/bin
   
g) Include the execution directory in the PATH (if it was not already).
   For the C shell for example, edit $home/.cshrc and add the line
   
   setenv PATH $PATH:$home/bin
   
   Update the path hash table (C shell) with the command
   
   rehash

h) Set the environment variable SCOREDIR to the execution directory
   
   setenv SCOREDIR $home/bin
   
   This can be set in the same shell configuration file, e.g. $home/.cshrc
   for the C shell.
   
   Alternatively, the SCOREDIR variable can be passed directly to the program
   each time it is started using the command "env":
   
   env SCOREDIR=$home/bin SCORE -c 42 37kf1cn1.igc
   
